    case_topic: ["Life Cycle Costing", "Product Life Cycle", "Introduction", "Growth", "Maturity", "Decline"],
    caseText: `<p>Vertex Mobility launched a compact electric scooter called VoltX. During the first year, sales were relatively low and the company spent heavily on advertising, dealer incentives and customer education. The product had few direct competitors at the time, but management knew that customers were still unfamiliar with the technology.</p>

    <p>During the second and third years, customer awareness increased rapidly. Sales volumes grew substantially and additional dealers began stocking the scooter. Several competitors entered the market. Vertex continued spending on promotion but shifted some of its attention from simply creating awareness to strengthening brand identity and encouraging repeat purchases.</p>

    <p>By the sixth year, sales had reached a high level but growth had slowed. Competitors offered similar scooters, price competition increased and customers began comparing features more closely. Vertex introduced improved versions and offered dealer incentives to maintain market share.</p>

    <p>Several years later, sales started falling. A newer generation of scooters offered longer battery life and faster charging. The number of companies producing the original type of scooter declined. Management had to decide whether to continue selling VoltX to a smaller loyal customer segment, reduce the price, introduce new features or discontinue the product.</p>

    <p>The finance department prepared a life cycle cost report covering research and development, manufacturing, advertising, distribution, warranty and end-of-life costs. The CFO explained that evaluating only the current year's manufacturing cost would provide an incomplete picture of the product's profitability.</p>

    <p>The product manager argued that the report was particularly useful because some costs had been incurred long before significant sales revenue appeared. The finance department agreed and noted that product life cycle costing allows costs and revenues to be considered over the product's entire life rather than only within individual accounting periods.</p>`,

