    caseText: `
      <p>Nova Appliances Ltd. is planning to launch a new energy-efficient kitchen appliance called the
      <strong>Nova SmartHeat</strong>. The company operates in a highly competitive consumer-appliance
      market where customers are sensitive to both selling price and product features. Management believes
      that simply adding a conventional profit margin to the estimated manufacturing cost would result in
      a selling price that is significantly higher than that of competitors.</p>

      <p>After conducting market research, the marketing department estimated that customers would be
      willing to pay approximately <strong>₹8,000 per unit</strong> for the proposed product. The company
      intends to earn a profit of <strong>20% of the selling price</strong>. Management therefore instructed
      the design and cost-management team to determine the maximum cost at which the product should be
      manufactured.</p>

      <p>The initial engineering design produced the following estimated cost per unit: direct material
      ₹3,200, direct labour ₹1,500, variable manufacturing overhead ₹900 and other product-related
      costs ₹700. The proposed product would therefore require substantial cost reduction before entering
      production.</p>

      <p>A cross-functional team consisting of personnel from design, production, purchasing, marketing
      and finance was formed. During value engineering, the team discovered that a particular metal
      component costing ₹600 could be replaced by a standardised component costing ₹450 without affecting
      the intended functionality or durability. The replacement would also reduce the number of different
      components maintained in inventory.</p>

      <p>The team further identified another opportunity. A redesign of the assembly process could reduce
      direct labour by 10%, although the redesign would increase variable manufacturing overhead by
      ₹40 per unit. Management decided that the redesigned process would be implemented if it reduced
      overall unit cost without impairing product quality.</p>
    `,
