    caseText: `
    <p>PurePack Ltd. manufactures specialised packaging film. The company has recently received pressure
    from customers to reduce its environmental footprint. Management initially believed that its
    environmental costs were limited to ₹15 lakh spent annually on pollution monitoring, compliance and
    waste-treatment equipment.</p>
    <p>An environmental management accounting study produced a different picture. During the year,
    the company purchased <strong>20,000 tonnes</strong> of polymer material at an average cost of
    ₹40,000 per tonne. Only 16,500 tonnes were incorporated into saleable packaging film. Of the
    remaining 3,500 tonnes, 1,500 tonnes were recyclable scrap, 1,000 tonnes represented process loss
    and 1,000 tonnes required hazardous-waste treatment.</p>
    <p>The company incurred ₹6 crore in direct material purchases. Waste treatment cost was ₹35 lakh,
    environmental monitoring cost was ₹15 lakh and an additional ₹10 lakh was spent on environmental
    audits and compliance reporting.</p>
    <p>The management accountant explained that simply recording the ₹60 lakh environmental expenditure
    would understate the economic significance of material inefficiency. Material losses consume
    resources and may generate environmental costs even when they do not appear separately in the
    general ledger.</p>
    <p>The production director proposed increasing production speed to improve labour efficiency.
    However, the environmental manager argued that doing so without addressing material-flow losses
    could increase waste and environmental costs. The board therefore requested an analysis based on
    both physical and monetary information.</p>
    `,
