    caseText: `
    <p>Ardent Home Systems Ltd. manufactures premium water-purification systems for residential
    customers. The company has traditionally followed a cost-plus approach. Engineers first developed
    the product, the production department estimated the manufacturing cost, and the finance department
    subsequently added a desired profit margin to determine the selling price.</p>
    <p>The approach had worked when the company faced limited competition. However, several international
    manufacturers have recently entered the market with technologically similar products. Customers now
    compare prices, filtration capacity, replacement-filter costs, warranty coverage and electricity
    consumption before making a purchase. The marketing department therefore conducted a detailed market
    study before the next model was designed.</p>
    <p>The study indicated that customers were willing to pay approximately <strong>₹24,000</strong>
    for the proposed purifier. Management wanted a profit equal to <strong>25% of the selling price</strong>.
    The design team prepared an initial cost estimate consisting of direct materials of ₹10,200,
    direct labour of ₹4,800, variable production overhead of ₹2,400, packaging of ₹1,100 and
    distribution and installation costs of ₹1,700 per unit.</p>
    <p>During the design review, the value-engineering team discovered that the outer casing used a
    specially manufactured alloy. The casing cost ₹2,400 per unit. A standardised polymer-composite
    casing costing ₹1,750 could provide the required appearance and durability. Testing indicated that
    the proposed substitute would not reduce the expected useful life of the product.</p>
    <p>The team also discovered that the current assembly arrangement required 48 minutes of direct
    labour. By redesigning the internal arrangement, assembly time could be reduced by 6 minutes.
    The direct labour rate was ₹600 per hour. However, the redesign would require an additional
    inspection activity costing ₹120 per unit.</p>
    <p>Management wanted the design team to determine whether the proposed changes were sufficient to
    maintain the required profitability without compromising customer-required functionality. The
    finance director specifically warned the team not to treat every cost reduction proposal as
    desirable merely because it reduced accounting cost.</p>
    `,
