    caseText: `
    <p>EverGreen Chemicals Ltd. manufactures industrial cleaning chemicals. The company has historically
    considered environmental expenditure primarily as a compliance issue. Its annual environmental
    budget included pollution monitoring, waste treatment and regulatory reporting. However, the board
    recently discovered that the company was spending considerably more on raw materials than competitors
    producing similar quantities of finished chemicals.</p>
    <p>An environmental management accounting investigation was therefore undertaken. During one quarter,
    the plant purchased <strong>12,000 tonnes</strong> of chemical input at an average cost of
    <strong>₹50,000 per tonne</strong>. Of this input, only 9,000 tonnes became saleable output.</p>
    <p>The remaining 3,000 tonnes consisted of 800 tonnes of recyclable material, 1,200 tonnes of
    process waste and 1,000 tonnes of hazardous residue. The company incurred ₹42 lakh on hazardous
    waste treatment and ₹18 lakh on environmental monitoring and compliance.</p>
    <p>The production team initially argued that only the ₹60 lakh spent directly on environmental
    activities should be reported as environmental cost. The management accountant disagreed. The
    physical material loss itself represents resource inefficiency and has financial consequences.</p>
    <p>The investigation also identified a separate potential external consequence. A historical
    leakage incident had contaminated a small area outside the plant boundary. The company had not yet
    incurred the full restoration cost, but environmental consultants estimated that cleanup could cost
    ₹25 lakh if the regulator required immediate restoration.</p>
    <p>The CFO asked the team to classify the relevant costs, identify the material-flow inefficiency and
    explain why environmental management accounting can influence decisions beyond statutory reporting.</p>
    `,
