    caseText: `
    <p>Silverline Consumer Products Ltd. is developing a new smart kitchen appliance. The engineering
    department has traditionally followed an inside-out approach to product development. Engineers
    first decide the technical specifications, calculate the expected production cost and then add a
    margin to arrive at a selling price.</p>
    <p>For the new product, however, the marketing department presented evidence that customers were
    comparing competing products primarily on energy efficiency, reliability, ease of cleaning and
    warranty support. Several technical features initially proposed by engineers were found to have
    little importance to customers.</p>
    <p>The management accountant proposed using target costing. The proposed market price would be
    established after considering competitor prices, expected demand, market positioning and customer
    willingness to pay. The desired profit margin would then be deducted from that market-driven price
    to determine the target cost.</p>
    <p>The engineering department objected that this approach would restrict its freedom because the
    product could not simply be designed to whatever cost engineers considered appropriate. The
    marketing manager responded that customers ultimately determine the value of the product and that
    the product design must therefore reflect customer requirements.</p>
    <p>A cross-functional team was subsequently formed involving marketing, design, production,
    purchasing, finance and service personnel. The team also invited important suppliers to participate
    because some major costs were embedded in purchased components rather than internal manufacturing.</p>
    <p>The finance director stressed that the objective was not simply to use cheaper components.
    Instead, the team had to search for better combinations of function, quality, reliability and cost.
    Any proposed design change had to be evaluated from the customer's perspective and from the total
    value-chain perspective.</p>
    `,
