    caseText: `
    <p>Omega Consumer Electronics Ltd. is launching a premium wireless speaker. The company expects
    strong competition throughout the product's commercial life. Management has therefore decided that
    cost management cannot stop once the initial product design is approved.</p>
    <p>Before launch, the engineering department performed a detailed design review. It identified several
    alternative materials and manufacturing arrangements. The team also examined whether certain
    components could be standardised with components already used in other products. Management believed
    that design decisions were particularly important because changes made after production started
    would be more disruptive.</p>
    <p>During the introduction stage, the company expects to spend heavily on advertising, product
    demonstrations and retailer incentives. Management does not expect high profit immediately because
    customers are still becoming aware of the product.</p>
    <p>During the growth stage, management expects increasing sales but also expects new competitors to
    enter the market. The company plans to improve software functionality and strengthen customer loyalty.</p>
    <p>During maturity, management expects the rate of sales growth to slow. Competitors may reduce prices
    and introduce alternative products. The company intends to improve selected features and seek
    additional users rather than immediately abandoning the product.</p>
    <p>Management expects production-stage cost pressure to continue even after the original value
    engineering exercise has been completed. The production manager therefore proposes small continuous
    improvements in material usage, assembly time, packaging and workplace organisation.</p>
    <p>The finance director asks whether these repeated small improvements should be considered another
    form of value engineering or whether they represent a distinct cost-management technique associated
    with the production stage.</p>
    `,
