    caseText: `

      <p>ValueMart Retail Limited operates a chain of supermarkets. The company
      recently realised that its product-level profitability reports were heavily
      influenced by gross margin and did not adequately reflect the resources
      consumed by individual products.</p>

      <p>The Finance Director therefore introduced a simplified Direct Product
      Profitability system. Three products are being reviewed: frozen meals,
      packaged pulses and premium shampoo.</p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Product</th>
            <th>Selling Price</th>
            <th>Purchase Cost</th>
            <th>Warehouse Cost</th>
            <th>Transport Cost</th>
            <th>Store Cost</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Frozen Meals</td>
            <td>₹150</td>
            <td>₹90</td>
            <td>₹10</td>
            <td>₹12</td>
            <td>₹15</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Packaged Pulses</td>
            <td>₹100</td>
            <td>₹68</td>
            <td>₹4</td>
            <td>₹6</td>
            <td>₹8</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Premium Shampoo</td>
            <td>₹180</td>
            <td>₹105</td>
            <td>₹6</td>
            <td>₹8</td>
            <td>₹12</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>The Retail Manager argues that frozen meals are the strongest product because
      customers purchase them regularly and the gross margin is ₹60 per unit. The
      Finance Director points out that frozen products consume refrigeration,
      specialised transportation and additional store resources.</p>

      <p>The management team therefore wants to calculate DPP rather than relying only
      on gross margin. The company is also considering whether premium shampoo should
      receive additional shelf space because its DPP may justify a larger allocation
      of scarce store resources.</p>

      <p>The Finance Director reminds the team that a lower-DPP product may still be
      strategically necessary to maintain a complete range and satisfy customer
      expectations.</p>
    `,
