    difficulty: "hard",
    caseText: `<p>Zenith Consumer Products Limited is a diversified manufacturer with two investment divisions, HomeCare and PersonalCare. The board noticed that both divisions reported strong accounting performance, but the chief financial officer suspected that managers were taking actions specifically designed to improve annual performance indicators. HomeCare had an existing investment base of ₹300 crore and controllable profit of ₹48 crore. Its manager had been offered a new automated production line costing ₹60 crore that was expected to generate additional annual controllable profit of ₹8.4 crore. The company's required return was 10%. The HomeCare manager argued against the investment because the project's ROI was only 14%, below the division's current 16% ROI. At the same time, he proposed selling an older machine near year-end. The machine generated only ₹1 crore of annual profit but had a book value of ₹20 crore. The sale would reduce the division's asset base and immediately improve its reported ROI. PersonalCare had assets of ₹200 crore and controllable profit of ₹22 crore, giving it an ROI of 11%. Its manager accepted a project requiring ₹40 crore and expected to generate ₹4.8 crore of annual profit because the project would raise the division's ROI. However, the project return was only 12%, and management's required return remained 10%, so it was acceptable to the company. The CFO also reviewed corporate EVA. Zenith's operating profit before tax was ₹90 crore, tax rate 30%, and invested capital ₹600 crore. The market-value capital structure consisted of 70% equity and 30% debt. Cost of equity was 13%, and pre-tax cost of debt was 7%. The CFO pointed out that EVA would charge the business for both equity and debt capital, unlike ordinary accounting profit. Meanwhile, EPS had increased from ₹8.20 to ₹8.70 because of a reduction in the number of outstanding shares following a buyback, even though total operating profit had remained almost unchanged. The CFO warned that EPS improvement alone could create a misleading impression if managers focused on financial engineering rather than underlying operating performance. The board therefore decided to review ROI, RI, EVA, EPS and non-financial indicators together. It also introduced controls against delaying maintenance, cutting employee development, postponing necessary expenditure or selling productive assets solely to improve year-end ratios. The board's objective was to reduce short-termism and ensure that divisional incentives remained aligned with long-term shareholder value.`,
