    case_topic: ["EVA", "ROI", "Balanced Scorecard", "Quality", "Short-termism"],
    difficulty: "hard",
    caseText: `<p>National Precision Systems Limited is a diversified engineering group with two major divisions, Industrial Automation and Medical Equipment. The board recently discovered that both divisions were reporting acceptable financial results, but the quality of management decisions differed significantly. Industrial Automation had controllable profit of ₹75 crore and controllable investment of ₹500 crore, giving it an ROI of 15%. Medical Equipment had controllable profit of ₹42 crore and controllable investment of ₹350 crore, giving it an ROI of 12%. The corporate cost of capital was 10%. Industrial Automation's manager had rejected a proposed robotics project requiring ₹100 crore because it was expected to generate only ₹12 crore annual controllable profit. Although the project would generate 12%, the manager feared that accepting it would reduce the division's reported ROI. Medical Equipment's manager accepted a project requiring ₹80 crore that would generate ₹8.8 crore because it increased the division's ROI from 12% to approximately 12.1%. The CFO pointed out that the first project generated a return above the company's cost of capital, while the second also exceeded the hurdle rate only modestly. The board therefore reviewed residual income. It also asked the finance team to calculate corporate EVA. National Precision's operating profit before tax was ₹180 crore, tax rate 30%, and invested capital ₹1,200 crore. The capital structure consisted of 60% equity and 40% debt. Cost of equity was 14% and pre-tax cost of debt was 7%. The company had recently spent ₹30 crore on R&D and ₹20 crore on a long-term customer education campaign. Both expenditures had been charged substantially to the current year's accounting profit even though management expected benefits for future years. At the same time, the company had reduced preventive maintenance expenditure, causing machine downtime to increase by 35%. Customer complaints in Medical Equipment increased by 22%, while employee turnover among skilled engineers rose from 8% to 13%. The board decided to build a Balanced Scorecard covering financial, customer, internal process and learning-and-growth perspectives. It also introduced quality KPIs and sustainability indicators. The CFO explained that a sophisticated performance system should not simply replace ROI with EVA or financial measures with non-financial measures. Instead, the system should combine appropriate indicators and ensure that managers cannot improve one measure through behaviour that damages long-term value. The board therefore linked divisional incentives to financial value creation, customer outcomes, process quality, employee capability and strategic objectives. It also required major investment proposals to show their effect on ROI, RI, EVA and relevant non-financial measures before approval.</p>`,
