    difficulty: "hard",
    caseText: `<p>NovaMed Technologies Limited manufactures advanced medical diagnostic equipment and operates through two major divisions, Diagnostic Systems and Patient Monitoring. The company had reported strong financial performance for several years, but the board became concerned that divisional incentives were encouraging short-term decisions. Diagnostic Systems had controllable profit of ₹72 crore and controllable investment of ₹480 crore, giving it an ROI of 15%. Patient Monitoring had controllable profit of ₹39 crore and controllable investment of ₹300 crore, giving it an ROI of 13%. The corporate required return was 10%. Diagnostic Systems was offered an automation project requiring ₹80 crore and expected to generate ₹10.4 crore annual controllable profit. The divisional manager opposed the project because its 13% return was below the division's existing 15% ROI. Patient Monitoring was offered a project requiring ₹50 crore and expected to generate ₹6.5 crore annual controllable profit. Its manager supported the project because the return was above the division's current ROI and would improve the reported percentage. The CFO explained that both projects generated returns above the corporate hurdle rate and therefore deserved evaluation from the company's perspective. The board then reviewed EVA. NovaMed's operating profit before tax was ₹210 crore, the tax rate was 30%, and opening invested capital was ₹1,500 crore. Market-value capital structure consisted of 65% equity and 35% debt. Cost of equity was 14%, and pre-tax cost of debt was 8%. During the year, the company spent ₹40 crore on R&D for a new diagnostic platform and ₹15 crore on long-term customer education. Management expected both expenditures to provide benefits over several years, although accounting treatment charged significant amounts to current profit. The board also discovered that preventive maintenance expenditure had been reduced by ₹6 crore, resulting in machine downtime increasing by 29%. Customer complaints increased by 19%, while the turnover of specialised engineers increased from 7% to 13%. At the same time, the company's operating profit increased by 10%, creating an apparently positive financial picture. The board decided to introduce a Balanced Scorecard covering financial performance, customer outcomes, internal processes and learning and growth. It also introduced Triple Bottom Line indicators covering employee welfare and development under People, environmental impact of manufacturing under Planet, and economic performance under Profit. Quality KPIs included first-pass yield, warranty claims and customer complaints. The CFO explained that no single measure could capture the complete performance of NovaMed. ROI could create divisional sub-optimisation, EVA could provide a better economic-value perspective but remained an absolute measure, and financial measures could fail to reveal deterioration in customer service, employee capability or sustainability. The board therefore decided that investment proposals would be assessed using ROI, RI and EVA together with strategic and non-financial indicators. Managers would also be prevented from improving short-term financial performance by cutting essential maintenance, employee training or product-quality expenditure.</p>`,
