  case_topic: ["CSF", "KPI", "Responsibility Accounting", "SMART Targets", "Performance Measurement"],
  difficulty: "medium",
  caseText: `<p>Arden Home Systems Limited manufactures premium kitchen appliances such as built-in ovens, induction cooktops, refrigerators and smart dishwashers. The company operates through three major divisions: Manufacturing, Sales & Distribution and Customer Service. During the previous financial year, the board observed that total sales had increased by 14%, but customer complaints had also increased significantly. The Managing Director therefore asked the management accountant to redesign the performance measurement system. The review showed that the Manufacturing Division was mainly evaluated on production volume and total manufacturing cost, while the Sales Division was evaluated almost entirely on sales revenue. The Customer Service Division was evaluated on the number of complaints closed during the month. Management realised that these measures were creating undesirable behaviour. Manufacturing supervisors were encouraging workers to increase output even when quality inspection capacity was insufficient. Sales managers were offering discounts to achieve monthly revenue targets, reducing contribution margins. Customer Service employees were closing complaints quickly without always resolving the underlying customer problem. After interviewing customers, employees and divisional managers, the company identified three critical success factors. The first was consistent product quality, because repeat purchases and warranty costs were strongly affected by defects. The second was reliable delivery, because dealers had complained about delayed shipments. The third was effective after-sales service, because customers increasingly expected rapid resolution of technical problems. Management proposed several KPIs. For quality, it proposed the percentage of units passing final inspection without rework, with a target of 97%. For delivery, it proposed the percentage of dealer orders delivered within the promised date, with a target of 95%. For customer service, it proposed the percentage of technical complaints resolved within 48 hours, with a target of 90%. The finance team suggested that each KPI should have a clearly defined data source and that managers should be evaluated only on factors over which they had reasonable control. The company also changed its responsibility structure. Manufacturing became a cost centre, Sales & Distribution became a revenue/profit responsibility centre depending on the decision involved, and Customer Service remained a cost centre but was given specific service-quality targets. The Managing Director insisted that the new targets should be SMART and should not encourage managers to sacrifice long-term performance for short-term results. During the first quarter, Manufacturing achieved 98% first-pass quality, Sales delivered 92% of dealer orders on time, and Customer Service resolved 94% of technical complaints within 48 hours. However, Sales management argued that the delivery target was partly outside its control because production delays affected availability. The board therefore decided that the delivery KPI should be jointly reviewed with Manufacturing rather than assigning the entire responsibility to Sales.`,
