  difficulty: "medium",
  caseText: `<p>EverGreen Packaging Solutions Limited manufactures food and pharmaceutical packaging for large consumer-goods companies. The company had previously considered profitability to be the primary indicator of success. However, several important customers began asking suppliers to demonstrate environmental and social responsibility. The board therefore adopted a Triple Bottom Line approach covering Profit, People and Planet. During the strategic review, management identified several initiatives. The company planned to install solar panels at its largest warehouse, replace diesel-powered forklifts with electric forklifts, reduce plastic waste from production, and redesign packaging so that a greater proportion could be recycled. On the People side, it planned to improve workplace safety, introduce additional technical training, strengthen employee grievance mechanisms and audit suppliers for labour practices. It also planned to provide financial assistance to a local vocational training centre. On the Profit side, the company wanted to improve operating margins, increase shareholder returns and reduce avoidable production costs. The finance team initially argued that several environmental projects would reduce current profits because of the upfront investment. The CEO responded that sustainability decisions should not be judged solely by immediate accounting profit. The company also began tracking electricity consumption per unit of production, tonnes of waste sent to landfill, workplace accidents, employee training hours, supplier compliance and operating profit. After one year, electricity consumption per unit had fallen, landfill waste had decreased and employee training had increased. However, operating profit had fallen slightly because the company had invested heavily in the new equipment. The board therefore decided to assess whether the initiatives created a balanced outcome across all three dimensions rather than judging the programme solely on the current year's accounting profit.`,
